# Workstreams registry binding 1.0.0

Artifact Type: `interface-contract`
Artifact ID: `interface.workstreams-registry-binding`
Purpose: Bind signed Workstreams requests to a durable producer registry.
Governing artifact: accepted canonical-registry-identity proposal v1
Decision owner: Jones Systems
Consumers: control-plane producer and Jones Workstreams transport
Authority effect: none

<!-- codex-section:begin id="interface.workstreams-registry-binding#wire.001" -->
## Canonical token and transport

Protocol: `workstreams-registry-binding/1.0.0`. The token is unpadded base64url
of UTF-8 JSON, no whitespace, in this exact key order: `protocol`,
`manifest_sha256`, `registry_instance_id`, `canonical_origin`, `owner_id`,
`principal_id`, `authorization_revision`. Reject duplicates, extra keys,
noncanonical encoding, invalid UTF-8 and decoded content over 2048 bytes.
The manifest digest is SHA-256 of exact `manifest.json` bytes. Its ordered file
entries hash exact specification and schema bytes. Vectors are separately pinned
by `vectors.sha256` and the source commit, avoiding a self-referential digest.

The instance ID is a canonical lowercase UUID. Origin is an exact canonical
HTTPS origin of at most 512 characters, with no credentials, path, trailing
slash, query or fragment. URL normalization must not change it. It is metadata,
never a destination or redirect. The actual transport URL remains separately
configured, including supported literal-loopback HTTP. Domain ownership and
uniqueness require explicit operator designation; syntax alone proves neither.
Independent registries require different instance IDs and origins. Existing
HTTPS registries should retain their client-visible origin. Restored copies of
the same logical registry retain identity under existing generation fences;
independent forks require separate provisioning. There is no clone operation.
Native provider `registry_origin` bytes and meaning remain unchanged.

Bound requests use `x-control-algorithm: hmac-sha256-workstreams-registry-v1`
and `x-control-registry-binding: <token>`. HMAC-SHA256 input is UTF-8 fields
joined by LF, no final LF: algorithm, request contract version, request ID,
idempotency key (empty if absent), timestamp, nonce, principal ID, key ID,
uppercase method, exact normalized request target, lowercase body SHA-256,
request contract manifest, exact token. All existing field validation applies.
Only existing signed Workstreams route families accept this algorithm:
`/workstreams/v1/`, T3 placement lookup, and appearance read/write. General APIs
reject it. Legacy canonical bytes remain unchanged. Removing or altering the
token or algorithm invalidates authentication. No downgrade or fallback exists.
<!-- codex-section:end id="interface.workstreams-registry-binding#wire.001" -->

<!-- codex-section:begin id="interface.workstreams-registry-binding#proof.001" -->
## Response proof and effect classification

After verified signature and nonce freshness, a bound response has
`x-control-registry-binding` (the exact request token),
`x-control-registry-dispatch` (`not-dispatched` or `dispatched`) and
`x-control-registry-response-mac` (canonical unpadded base64url HMAC-SHA256).
MAC input is UTF-8 LF-joined fields with no final LF:
`workstreams-registry-response/1.0.0`, request signature, decimal HTTP status,
response `x-control-contract-version` (empty if absent), response
`x-control-contract-manifest` (empty if absent), exact token, dispatch class,
lowercase SHA-256 of exact response bytes. Verify in constant time before DTO
parsing and enforce the family's existing response bound (at most 1 MiB).
Never learn or replace a pin from a response. The response MAC is request-bound.

Dispatch means entry to the business application, regardless of HTTP verb;
receipt GET polling may reconcile persisted state. Dispatch does not prove
commit. A not-dispatched proof is issued only when no business handler began.
Identity, required-policy or current-owner/grant mismatch after authentication
returns HTTP 409 with exactly `{"code":"registry_binding_not_current"}` and
`not-dispatched`. This envelope refusal is independent of existing DTO schemas.
The consumer MUST preserve command identity/provenance and map this verified
refusal to the existing `workstream_command_not_current` conflict for unchanged
clients (including grant changes after a successful capabilities read).

Missing/invalid proof or loss after a possible write is unknown-effect, retaining
original command/receipt for reconciliation. Never replay, fall back to legacy,
or report no-effect from HTTP status, GET verb, or unverified error text alone.
Valid proof of dispatch likewise cannot prove absence of an effect. Pre-signature
failures need not have a proof and must fail closed. Identity failure prohibits
gateway cached-success fallback and fresh authorization; existing explicitly
read-only stale display is outside this contract. No client-wide purge is claimed.
<!-- codex-section:end id="interface.workstreams-registry-binding#proof.001" -->

<!-- codex-section:begin id="interface.workstreams-registry-binding#provision.001" -->
## Durable provisioning and staging

Migration 014 adds singleton `workstream_registry_identity`, immutable required
`workstream_registry_binding_principals`, and a dedicated immutable
`workstream_registry_binding_requests` ledger. Runtime has SELECT only on all
three. Existing principal provisioning has no new ledger and is not retrofitted.
The closed provisioning request binds identity, owner, principal, generation,
active grant ID/revision, and exact nullable identity/policy preimages. Existing
identity preimage is `{registry_instance_id,canonical_origin}`; policy preimage
is `{registry_instance_id,owner_id,principal_id,mode:"required"}`.

`registry-binding-plan`, `registry-binding-apply`, `registry-binding-readback`
are separate provisioning CLI modes. Apply uses a serializable transaction,
fixed registry lock, current active principal/unique grant/context validation,
exact preimages, and a durable request ID plus canonical request SHA-256.
Request digest input is UTF-8 compact JSON (no trailing newline), with object
keys sorted recursively in ascending code-unit order and array order retained.
Identity and policy are insert-only. Exact replay requires the same recorded
request digest, exact current postimages, and current authority. Reusing an ID
with changed input conflicts. Different requests cannot bypass stale preimages.
Readback requires the durable request record AND postimages; a computed digest
alone is never evidence of application. Unknown COMMIT is never retried by the
source: read back the original request before deciding any further action.
A known successful COMMIT followed by failed readback remains unknown to caller.
No origin replacement, deletion, downgrade, or additional grant is exposed.

`stage-jones --registry-binding-version 1.0.0` reads identity/policy from the
same database as principal grant readback. It takes no replacement origin.
The optional `T3_WORKSTREAM_REGISTRY_BINDING` line follows the six existing
activation values and optional registration-context selector. Omission retains
an existing binding selection but refreshes it from durable current authority;
it never silently removes it. Existing identity cannot change on restaging.
Changed files require the existing exact SHA-256 preimage and same identity/key
lines; readback must match. No operational staging occurs as part of source tests.
<!-- codex-section:end id="interface.workstreams-registry-binding#provision.001" -->

<!-- codex-section:begin id="interface.workstreams-registry-binding#compatibility.001" -->
## Compatibility and operating boundary

Old/old remains unchanged, including the known loopback client serialization
failure. New producer/old Jones remains legacy until principal opt-in; required
principals reject legacy signed Workstreams requests before business dispatch.
Old producer/new Jones accepts legacy HTTPS but rejects the new algorithm before
business; bound clients fail closed. New/new uses canonical metadata with the
configured HTTPS or literal-loopback transport. Existing client binding schemas
remain unchanged. No installed version, real domain, enrollment, private state,
physical-host result or deployment is established by source fixtures.

Operator Interaction Inventory: source builds and synthetic tests require none.
Live schema apply requires exact database/backup/migration authority. Identity
and policy provisioning requires an explicit real origin designation and exact
migration-role authority. Private staging, installation and restart require their
separate approved file/host routes. Normal qualified bound requests need no
operator interaction. Unknown provisioning or staging outcomes require exact
readback, never automatic replay. Old binaries may reject database-ahead state;
qualify rollback against exact binaries, never delete identity/policy/history to
make rollback pass. Physical/native acceptance remains external to this repair.
<!-- codex-section:end id="interface.workstreams-registry-binding#compatibility.001" -->
